# Known Archon bugs (not stylistic Postgres differences) — these entries
# are asserted to *keep* diverging from correct behavior. If one of these
# is ever fixed, its test should move to supported/ rather than being
# silently updated in place, so the fix is visible in the diff. See
# ddl.slt (in supported/) for the corpus format convention, including the
# note on `DbError`'s Debug format for `statement error` patterns.
#
# Per Slice-1 spec fact #2, this is a real bug in the engine, not a gap —
# do not attempt it against real Postgres (the out-archon-pgcompat oracle
# crate skips divergent/ entirely; see its own doc comment).
#
# Facts #1 and #6, formerly tracked in this file, are fixed: CREATE TABLE
# now rejects a user column named "id" outright instead of silently
# colliding with the implicit row-id column, and the lexer now honors
# SQL-standard doubled-quote escaping. Their tests moved to
# supported/ddl.slt per this file's own move-don't-edit-in-place rule above.

# --- Fact #2 (narrowed): a qualifier for a table not in this join
# silently falls back to matching by trailing segment, instead of erroring
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Originally described as "qualified JOIN ON columns are broken" — that's
# no longer accurate: a qualifier naming a REAL table in this join (either
# side) now resolves via exact "table.col" matching (see supported/joins.slt
# for the fixed case, and `run_select_scoped`'s `on_cols` comment in
# select.rs). What's left: `find_value` (executor/expr.rs) still resolves
# an unmatched qualified name by stripping to its trailing segment and
# matching ANY same-suffix column in scope, without checking whether the
# qualifier the caller actually wrote names a real table here at all. Below,
# "bogus" isn't a table in this query, but `t3.v` exists — the trailing-
# segment fallback silently treats `bogus.v` as if it meant `t3.v` instead
# of raising an error for the unrecognized qualifier. Not tightened further
# here because `find_value`'s same fallback is also relied on (deliberately)
# for aliased correlated-subquery resolution elsewhere — see
# `Database::column_resolves_locally`'s doc comment in subquery.rs, which
# already avoids this exact fallback for ITS purpose, showing the fix is
# know-how, not unknown-how; just not safe to apply inside the shared
# `find_value` without auditing every caller.
statement ok
CREATE TABLE t3 (v INT)

statement ok
CREATE TABLE t4 (v INT)

statement ok
INSERT INTO t3 (v) VALUES (1)

statement ok
INSERT INTO t4 (v) VALUES (1)

query I
SELECT v FROM t3 JOIN t4 ON bogus.v = t4.v
----
1
